\fs96 \cf2 Functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo uses the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to define functions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb14 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 fn
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  keyword is deprecated as of Mojo v26.2. Use 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 def
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  for all function declarations. The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 fn
\fs28 \cf2 \cb14  keyword will be removed in a future release.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 Functions declared inside a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb15 \ul struct}} are called "methods," but they have all the same qualities as "functions" described here.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Anatomy of a function\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You'll find these elements in Mojo functions:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf10 \cb17 def function_name[\
\uc0\u8203     parameters ...\
](\
\uc0\u8203     arguments ...\
) -> return_value_type:\
\uc0\u8203     function_body\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Functions can have:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Parameters: A function can optionally take one or more compile-time parameter values used for metaprogramming.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Arguments: A function can also optionally take one or more run-time arguments.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Return value: A function can optionally return a value.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Function body: Statements that are executed when you call the function. Function definitions must include a body.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 All of the optional parts of the function can be omitted, so the minimal function is something like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 do_nothing\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 pass\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If a function takes no parameters, you can omit the square brackets, but the parentheses are always required.\
Although you can't leave out the function body, you can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pass
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement to define a function that does nothing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Arguments and parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Functions take two kinds of inputs: arguments and parameters. Arguments are familiar from many other languages: they are run-time values passed into the function.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 add\cf5 (\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  a\cf5 +\cf18 b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 On the other hand, you can think of a parameter as a compile-time variable that becomes a run-time constant. For example, consider the following function with a parameter:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  add_tensors\cf5 [\cf18 rank\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ](\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  MyTensor\cf5 [\cf18 rank\cf5 ],\cf18  b\cf5 :\cf18  MyTensor\cf5 [\cf18 rank\cf5 ])\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  MyTensor\cf5 [\cf18 rank\cf5 ]:\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this case, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rank
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value needs to be specified in a way that can be determined at compilation time, such as a literal or expression.\
When you compile a program that uses this code, the compiler produces a unique version of the function for each unique 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rank
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value used in the program, with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 rank
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  treated as a constant within each specialized version.\
This usage of "parameter" is probably different from what you're used to from other languages, where "parameter" and "argument" are often used interchangeably. In Mojo, "parameter" and "parameter expression" refer to compile-time values, and "argument" and "expression" refer to run-time values.\
By default, both arguments and parameters can be specified either by position or by keyword. These forms can also be mixed in the same function call.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # positional\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \cb17 x \cf5 =\cf18  add\cf5 (\cf22 5\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 7\cf5 )\cf18       \cf6 # Positionally, a=5 and b=7\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb17 # keyword\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \cb17 y \cf5 =\cf18  add\cf5 (\cf18 b\cf5 =\cf22 3\cf5 ,\cf18  a\cf5 =\cf22 9\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb17 # mixed\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \cb17 z \cf5 =\cf18  add\cf5 (\cf22 5\cf5 ,\cf18  b\cf5 =\cf22 7\cf5 )\cf18     \cf6 # Positionally, a=5\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information on arguments, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Function arguments\cf2 \ulnone  on this page. For more information on parameters, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Parameterization: compile-time metaprogramming}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Function requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A function has the following requirements:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You must declare the type of each function parameter and argument.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If a function doesn't return a value, you can either omit the return type or declare 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 None
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as the return type.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # The following function definitions are equivalent\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  \cf19 greet\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf5 print(\cf23 "Hello,"\cf18  name\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  \cf19 greet\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  \cf22 None\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf5 print(\cf23 "Hello,"\cf18  name\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf18 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf20 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf21 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If the function returns a value, you must either declare the return type using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -> type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax or provide a \cf5 \ul named result\cf2 \ulnone  in the argument list.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # The following function definitions are equivalent\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  \cf19 incr\cf5 (\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf5 return\cf18  a \cf5 +\cf18  \cf22 1\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  \cf19 incr\cf5 (\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 out\cf18  b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf18 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  b \cf5 =\cf18  a \cf5 +\cf18  \cf22 1\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf18 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf20 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf21 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 For more information, see the \cf5 \ul Return values\cf2 \ulnone  section of this page.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Function arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 Functions with / and * in the argument list\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb16 You might see the following characters in place of arguments: slash (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 /
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 ) and/or star (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 *
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 ). For example:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf18  \cf19 myfunc\cf5 (\cf18 pos_only\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 /,\cf18  pos_or_keyword\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 *,\cf18  keyword_only\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 Arguments before the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 /
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  can be passed only by position. Arguments after the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 *
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  can be passed only by keyword. For details, see \ul Positional-only and keyword-only arguments\cb1 \ulnone \
\cb16 You may also see argument names prefixed with one or two stars (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 *
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 ):\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf18  \cf19 myfunc2\cf5 (*\cf18 names\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 **\cf18 attributes\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb16 An argument name prefixed by a single star character, like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 *names
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  identifies a \ul variadic argument\ulnone , while an argument name prefixed with a double star, like 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 **attributes
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  identifies a \ul variadic keyword-only argument\ulnone .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Optional arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 An optional argument is one that includes a default value, such as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 exp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument here:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 my_pow\cf5 (\cf18 base\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  exp\cf5 :\cf18  Int \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 2\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  base \cf5 **\cf18  exp\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 use_defaults\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # Uses the default value for `exp`\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  z \cf5 =\cf18  my_pow\cf5 (\cf22 3\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf18 z\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, you can't define a default value for an argument that's declared with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#mutable-arguments-mut"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul mut}} argument convention.\
Any optional arguments must appear after any required arguments. \cf5 \ul Keyword-only arguments\cf2 \ulnone , discussed later, can also be either required or optional.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Keyword arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can also use keyword arguments when calling a function. Keyword arguments are specified using the format 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 argument_name = argument_value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can pass keyword arguments in any order:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 my_pow\cf5 (\cf18 base\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  exp\cf5 :\cf18  Int \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 2\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  base \cf5 **\cf18  exp\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 use_keywords\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # Uses keyword argument names (with order reversed)\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  z \cf5 =\cf18  my_pow\cf5 (\cf18 exp\cf5 =\cf22 3\cf5 ,\cf18  base\cf5 =\cf22 2\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf18 z\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Variadic arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Variadic arguments let a function accept a variable number of arguments. To define a function that takes a variadic argument, use the variadic argument syntax 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *argument_name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf5 (*\cf18 values\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 var\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf5 :\cf18  Int \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 0\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 for\cf18  value \cf5 in\cf18  values\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf19 sum\cf18  \cf5 =\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf18  \cf5 +\cf18  value\cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 return\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The variadic argument 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 values
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  here is a placeholder that accepts any number of passed positional arguments.\
You can define zero or more arguments before the variadic argument. When calling the function, any remaining positional arguments are assigned to the variadic argument, so any arguments declared after the variadic argument can only be specified by keyword (see \cf5 \ul Positional-only and keyword-only arguments\cf2 \ulnone ).\
Variadic arguments can be divided into two categories:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Homogeneous variadic arguments, where all of the passed arguments are the same type\'97all 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or all 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , for example.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Heterogeneous variadic arguments, which can accept a set of different argument types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The following sections describe how to work with homogeneous and heterogeneous variadic arguments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 Variadic parameters\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb16 Mojo also supports variadic parameters, but with some limitations\'97for details see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#variadic-parameters"}}{\fldrslt \ul variadic parameters}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Homogeneous variadic arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When defining a homogeneous variadic argument (all arguments must be the same type), use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *argument_name: argument_type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 greet\cf5 (*\cf18 names\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Inside the function body, the variadic argument is available as an iterable list for ease of use. Concretely, that type is named {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/variadics/VariadicList/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul VariadicList}}. Here is A simple example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf5 (*\cf18 values\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 var\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf5 :\cf18  Int \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 0\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 for\cf18  value \cf5 in\cf18  values\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf19 sum\cf18  \cf5 =\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf5 +\cf18 value\cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 return\cf18  \cf19 sum\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Iterating over this list directly with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for..in
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop currently produces a reference to the element, which can be mutable with a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variadic list. Use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  binding pattern to capture a mutable reference if you want to mutate the elements of the list:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 make_worldly\cf5 (mut\cf18  \cf5 *\cf18 strs\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 for\cf18  \cf5 ref\cf18  i \cf5 in\cf18  strs\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         i \cf5 +=\cf18  \cf23 " world"\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also directly index the list with integers as well:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 make_worldly\cf5 (mut\cf18  \cf5 *\cf18 strs\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 for\cf18  i \cf5 in\cf18  \cf19 range\cf5 (\cf19 len\cf5 (\cf18 strs\cf5 )):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         strs\cf5 [\cf18 i\cf5 ]\cf18  \cf5 +=\cf18  \cf23 " world"\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Heterogeneous variadic arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Implementing heterogeneous variadic arguments (each argument type may be different) is somewhat more complicated than homogeneous variadic arguments. To handle multiple argument types, the function must be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/generics/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul generic}}, which requires using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul traits}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul parameters}}. So the syntax may look a little unfamiliar if you haven't worked with those features.\
The signature for a function with a heterogeneous variadic argument looks like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  count_many_things\cf5 [*\cf18 ArgTypes\cf5 :\cf18  Intable\cf5 ](*\cf18 args\cf5 :\cf18  \cf5 *\cf18 ArgTypes\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The parameter list, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 [*ArgTypes: Intable]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  specifies that the function takes an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter, which is a list of types, all of which conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/int/Intable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Intable}} trait. The asterisk in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  indicates that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a variadic type parameter (a list of types).\
The argument list, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 (*args: *ArgTypes)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has the familiar 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for the variadic argument, but instead of a single type, its type is defined as the variadic type list 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . The asterisk in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  indicates a variadic argument, and the asterisk in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  refers to the variadic type parameter.\
This means that each argument in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has a corresponding type in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 args[n]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArgTypes[n]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Inside the function, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  becomes a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/variadics/VariadicPack/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul VariadicPack}} because the syntax 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *args: *ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  creates a heterogeneous variadic argument. That means each element in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 args
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be a different type that requires a different amount of memory. To iterate through the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 VariadicPack
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the compiler must know each element's type, so you must use a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/metaprogramming/comptime-evaluation/#comptime-for"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime for
\fs32 \cb1  loop}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  count_many_things\cf5 [*\cf18 ArgTypes\cf5 :\cf18  Intable\cf5 ](*\cf18 args\cf5 :\cf18  \cf5 *\cf18 ArgTypes\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  total \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 0\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 comptime\cf18  \cf5 for\cf18  i \cf5 in\cf18  \cf19 range\cf5 (\cf18 args\cf5 .\cf18 __len__\cf5 ()):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         total \cf5 +=\cf18  Int\cf5 (\cf18 args\cf5 [\cf18 i\cf5 ])\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  total\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 main\cf5 ()\cf18  raises\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf18 count_many_things\cf5 (\cf22 5\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 11.7\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 12\cf5 ))\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf18 \cb17 28\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice that when calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 count_many_things()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you don't actually pass in a list of argument types. You only need to pass in the arguments, and Mojo generates the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ArgTypes
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  list itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Variadic keyword arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo functions also support variadic keyword arguments (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **kwargs
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Variadic keyword arguments allow the user to pass an arbitrary number of keyword arguments. To define a function that takes a variadic keyword argument, use the variadic keyword argument syntax 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **kw_argument_name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 print_nicely\cf5 (**\cf18 kwargs\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17   \cf5 for\cf18  key \cf5 in\cf18  kwargs\cf5 .\cf18 keys\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17       \cf5 print(\cf18 key\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf23 "="\cf5 ,\cf18  kwargs\cf5 [\cf18 key\cf5 ])\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17  \cf6 # prints:\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17  \cf6 # `a = 7`\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17  \cf6 # `y = 8`\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17 print_nicely\cf5 (\cf18 a\cf5 =\cf22 7\cf5 ,\cf18  y\cf5 =\cf22 8\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, the argument name 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 kwargs
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a placeholder that accepts any number of keyword arguments. Inside the body of the function, you can access the arguments as a dictionary of keywords and argument values (specifically, an instance of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/OwnedKwargsDict/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul OwnedKwargsDict}}).\
There are currently a few limitations:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Variadic keyword arguments are always implicitly treated as if they were declared with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul argument convention}}, and can't be declared otherwise:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # Not supported yet.\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  \cf19 read_var_kwargs\cf5 (read\cf18  \cf5 **\cf18 kwargs\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18  \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf18 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf20 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf21 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
All the variadic keyword arguments must have the same type, and this determines the type of the argument dictionary. For example, if the argument is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 **kwargs: Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  then the argument dictionary will be a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 OwnedKwargsDict[Float64]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The argument type must conform to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/Copyable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul Copyable}} trait.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dictionary unpacking is not supported yet:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 takes_dict\cf5 (\cf18 d\cf5 :\cf18  Dict\cf5 [\cf18 String\cf5 ,\cf18  Int\cf5 ]):\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf18 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  print_nicely\cf5 (**\cf18 d\cf5 )\cf18   \cf6 # Not supported yet.\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf18 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf20 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf21 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Variadic keyword parameters are not supported yet:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # Not supported yet.\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf5 \cb17 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf18  var_kwparams\cf5 [**\cf18 kwparams\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ]():\cf18  \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf18 \cb17 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf20 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf21 \uc0\u8232 \cf20 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Positional-only and keyword-only arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When defining a function, you can restrict some arguments so that they can be passed only as positional arguments, or they can be passed only as keyword arguments.\
To define positional-only arguments, add a slash character (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to the argument list. Any arguments before the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are positional-only: they can't be passed as keyword arguments. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 min\cf5 (\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 /)\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  a \cf5 if\cf18  a \cf5 <\cf18  b \cf5 else\cf18  b\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function can be called with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min(1, 2)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  but can't be called using keywords, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min(a=1, b=2)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
There are several reasons you might want to write a function with positional-only arguments:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The argument names aren't meaningful for the caller.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You want the freedom to change the argument names later on without breaking backward compatibility.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 min()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, the argument names don't add any real information, and there's no reason to specify arguments by keyword.\
For more information on positional-only arguments, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peps.python.org/pep-0570/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul PEP 570 \'96 Python Positional-Only Parameters}}.\
Keyword-only arguments are the inverse of positional-only arguments: they can be specified only by keyword. If a function accepts variadic arguments, any arguments defined after the variadic arguments are treated as keyword-only. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 sort\cf5 (*\cf18 values\cf5 :\cf18  Float64\cf5 ,\cf18  ascending\cf5 :\cf18  Bool \cf5 =\cf18  \cf22 True\cf5 ):\cf18  \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, the user can pass any number of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Float64
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values, optionally followed by the keyword 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ascending
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 var\cf18  a \cf5 =\cf18  sort\cf5 (\cf22 1.1\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 6.5\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 4.3\cf5 ,\cf18  ascending\cf5 =\cf22 False\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If the function doesn't accept variadic arguments, you can add a single star (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 *
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to the argument list to separate the keyword-only arguments:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 kw_only_args\cf5 (\cf18 a1\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  a2\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 *,\cf18  double\cf5 :\cf18  Bool\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  product \cf5 =\cf18  a1 \cf5 *\cf18  a2\cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 if\cf18  double\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 return\cf18  product \cf5 *\cf18  \cf22 2\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 else:\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 return\cf18  product\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Keyword-only arguments often have default values, but this is not required. If a keyword-only argument doesn't have a default value, it is a required keyword-only argument. It must be specified, and it must be specified by keyword.\
Any required keyword-only arguments must appear in the signature before any optional keyword-only arguments. That is, arguments appear in the following sequence in a function signature:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Required positional arguments.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Optional positional arguments.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Variadic arguments.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Required keyword-only arguments.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Optional keyword-only arguments.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Variadic keyword arguments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more information on keyword-only arguments, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://peps.python.org/pep-3102/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul PEP 3102 \'96 Keyword-Only Arguments}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Overloaded functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 All function declarations must specify argument types, so if you want a function to work with different data types, you need to implement separate versions of the function that each specify different argument types. This is called "overloading" a function.\
For example, here's an overloaded 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 add()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function that can accept either 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  types:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 add\cf5 (\cf18 x\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  y\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  x \cf5 +\cf18  y\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 add\cf5 (\cf18 x\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ,\cf18  y\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  String\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  x \cf5 +\cf18  y\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you pass anything other than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 add()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, you'll get a compiler error. That is, unless 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can implicitly cast the type into their own type. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  includes an overloaded version of its constructor (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) that supports {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructors-and-implicit-conversion"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul implicit conversion}} from a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value. Thus, you can also pass a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to a function that expects a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
When resolving an overloaded function call, the Mojo compiler tries each candidate function and uses the one that works (if only one version works), or it picks the closest match (if it can determine a close match), or it reports that the call is ambiguous (if it can't figure out which one to pick). For details on how Mojo picks the best candidate, see \cf5 \ul Overload resolution\cf2 \ulnone .\
If the compiler can't figure out which function to use, you can resolve the ambiguity by explicitly casting your value to a supported argument type. For example, the following code calls the overloaded 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 foo()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function, but both implementations accept an argument that supports {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructors-and-implicit-conversion"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul implicit conversion}} from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 StringLiteral
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . So, the call to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 foo(string)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is ambiguous and creates a compiler error. You can fix this by casting the value to the type you really want:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 struct\cf18  \cf19 MyString\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 @implicit\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (out\cf18  self\cf5 ,\cf18  string\cf5 :\cf18  StringLiteral\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 pass\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 foo\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "String"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 foo\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 :\cf18  MyString\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "MyString"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 call_foo\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 comptime\cf18  string \cf5 =\cf18  \cf23 "Hello"\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # foo(string) # error: ambiguous call to 'foo' ... This call is ambiguous because two `foo` functions match it\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     foo\cf5 (\cf18 MyString\cf5 (\cf18 string\cf5 ))\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 Overload Sets\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb16 An "overload set" is a collection of function overloads that share the same name but different signatures.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Overload sets can't be extended by imports, aliases, or parameters.\cb1 \
\ls10\ilvl0\cb16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If you define a function in a Mojo module, it hides any overload sets you imported earlier.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Overload resolution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When resolving an overloaded function, Mojo does not consider the return type or other contextual information at the call site\'97it considers only parameter and argument types and whether the functions are instance methods or static methods.\
The overload resolution logic filters for candidates according to the following rules, in order of precedence:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Candidates requiring the smallest number of implicit conversions (in both arguments and parameters).\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Candidates without variadic arguments.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Candidates without variadic parameters.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Candidates with the shortest parameter signature.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Non-
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @staticmethod
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  candidates (over 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @staticmethod
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  ones, if available).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If there is more than one candidate after applying these rules, the overload resolution fails. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 struct\cf18  \cf19 MyInt\cf5 (\cf18 TrivialRegisterPassable\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf23 """A type that is implicitly convertible to `Int`."""\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  value\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 @implicit\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (out\cf18  self\cf5 ,\cf18  _a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         self\cf5 .\cf18 value \cf5 =\cf18  _a\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  foo\cf5 [\cf18 x\cf5 :\cf18  MyInt\cf5 ,\cf18  a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ]():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "foo[x: MyInt, a: Int]()"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  foo\cf5 [\cf18 x\cf5 :\cf18  MyInt\cf5 ,\cf18  y\cf5 :\cf18  MyInt\cf5 ]():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "foo[x: MyInt, y: MyInt]()"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  bar\cf5 [\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ](\cf18 b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "bar[a: Int](b: Int)"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  bar\cf5 [\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ](*\cf18 b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "bar[a: Int](*b: Int)"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  bar\cf5 [*\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ](\cf18 b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 print(\cf23 "bar[*a: Int](b: Int)"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  parameter_overloads\cf5 [\cf18 a\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  b\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 ,\cf18  x\cf5 :\cf18  MyInt\cf5 ]():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # `foo[x: MyInt, a: Int]()` is called because it requires no implicit\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # conversions, whereas `foo[x: MyInt, y: MyInt]()` requires one.\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     foo\cf5 [\cf18 x\cf5 ,\cf18  a\cf5 ]()\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf6 # `bar[a: Int](b: Int)` is called because it does not have variadic\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # arguments or parameters.\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     bar\cf5 [\cf18 a\cf5 ](\cf18 b\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf6 # `bar[*a: Int](b: Int)` is called because it has variadic parameters.\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     bar\cf5 [\cf18 a\cf5 ,\cf18  a\cf5 ,\cf18  a\cf5 ](\cf18 b\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17 parameter_overloads\cf5 [\cf22 1\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf22 2\cf5 ,\cf18  MyInt\cf5 (\cf22 3\cf5 )]()\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 struct\cf18  \cf19 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (out\cf18  self\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 pass\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 foo\cf5 (mut\cf18  self\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 print(\cf23 "calling instance method"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 foo\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 print(\cf23 "calling static method"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 test_static_overload\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  a \cf5 =\cf18  MyStruct\cf5 ()\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # `foo(mut self)` takes precedence over a static method.\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     a\cf5 .\cf18 foo\cf5 ()\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf18 \cb17 foo[x: MyInt, a: Int]()\cb1 \
\cb17 bar[a: Int](b: Int)\cb1 \
\cb17 bar[*a: Int](b: Int)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Return values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Return value types are declared in the signature using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -> type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax. Values are passed using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword, which ends the function and returns the identified value (if any) to the caller.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 get_greeting\cf5 ()\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  String\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  \cf23 "Hello"\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By default, the value is returned to the caller as an owned value. As with arguments, a return value may be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructors-and-implicit-conversion"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul implicitly converted}} to the named return type. For example, the previous example calls 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with a string literal, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 "Hello"
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which is implicitly converted to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb16 Returning a reference\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb16 A function can also return a mutable or immutable reference using a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb15 ref
\fs28 \cf2 \cb16  return value. For details, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Lifetimes, origins, and references}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Named results\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Named function results allow a function to return a value that can't be moved or copied. Named result syntax lets you specify a named, uninitialized variable to return to the caller using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 get_name_tag\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 out\cf18  name_tag\cf5 :\cf18  NameTag\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     name_tag \cf5 =\cf18  NameTag\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 ^)\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument convention identifies an uninitialized variable that the function must initialize. (This is the same as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention used in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul struct constructors}}.) The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument for a named result can appear anywhere in the argument list, but by convention, it should be the last argument in the list.\
A function can declare only one return value, whether it's declared using an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument or using the standard 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -> type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax.\
A function with a named result argument doesn't need to include an explicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement, as shown above. If the function terminates without a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , or at a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement with no value, the value of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is returned to the caller. If it includes a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 return
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement with a value, that value is returned to the caller, as usual.\
The fact that a function uses a named result is transparent to the caller. That is, these two signatures are interchangeable to the caller:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 get_name_tag\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  NameTag\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 get_name_tag\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 out\cf18  name_tag\cf5 :\cf18  NameTag\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In both cases, the call looks like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf18 \cb17 tag \cf5 =\cf18  get_name_tag\cf5 (\cf23 "Judith"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because the return value is assigned to this special 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  variable, it doesn't need to be moved or copied when it's returned to the caller. This means that you can create a function that returns a type that can't be moved or copied, and which takes several steps to initialize:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 struct\cf18  \cf19 ImmovableObject\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cb1 \
\
\cb17     \cf5 def\cf18  \cf19 __init__\cf5 (out\cf18  self\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         self\cf5 .\cf18 name \cf5 =\cf18  name\cf5 ^\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 create_immovable_object\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 ,\cf18  \cf5 out\cf18  obj\cf5 :\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 ):\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     obj \cf5 =\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 ^)\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     obj\cf5 .\cf18 name \cf5 +=\cf18  \cf23 "!"\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf6 # obj is implicitly returned\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 main\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     my_obj \cf5 =\cf18  create_immovable_object\cf5 (\cf23 "Blob"\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By contrast, the following function with a standard return value doesn't work:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 create_immovable_object2\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     obj \cf5 =\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 ^)\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     obj\cf5 .\cf18 name \cf5 +=\cf18  \cf23 "!"\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  obj\cf5 ^\cf18  \cf6 # Error: ImmovableObject is not copyable or movable\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 create_immovable_object2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses a local variable to store the object while it's under construction, the return call requires it to be either moved or copied to the callee. This isn't an issue if the newly-created value is returned immediately:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 create_immovable_object3\cf5 (var\cf18  name\cf5 :\cf18  String\cf5 )\cf18  \cf5 ->\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 return\cf18  ImmovableObject\cf5 (\cf18 name\cf5 ^)\cf18  \cf6 # OK\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Raising and non-raising functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 By default, when a function raises an error, the function terminates immediately and the error propagates to the calling function. If the calling function doesn't handle the error, it continues to propagate up the call stack.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 raises_error\cf5 ()\cf18  raises\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 raise\cf18  Error\cf5 (\cf23 "There was an error."\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo functions are non-raising by default. To declare that a function can propagate an error to its caller, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 raises
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to the function signature. A non-raising function that calls a raising function must handle any possible errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb17 # This function will not compile\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 unhandled_error\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     raises_error\cf5 ()\cf18    \cf6 # Error: can't call raising function in a non-raising context\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb17 # Explicitly handle the error\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 handle_error\cf5 ():\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 try:\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         raises_error\cf5 ()\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 except\cf18  e\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17         \cf5 print(\cf23 "Handled an error:"\cf5 ,\cf18  e\cf5 )\cf18 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb17 # Explicitly propagate the error\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 propagate_error\cf5 ()\cf18  raises\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     raises_error\cf5 ()\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 All of the examples above use the built-in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Error
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type. Mojo also supports typed errors, where you specify a custom error type a function can raise:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb17 def\cf18  \cf19 validate\cf5 (\cf18 value\cf5 :\cf18  Int\cf5 )\cf18  raises ValidationError \cf5 ->\cf18  Int\cf5 :\cf18 \cb1 \
\cb17     \cf5 ...\cf18 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
